Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; Milwaukee working-class 1990s into later Seattle. Closed cast; first-person voice as NARRATOR.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

A fifth-floor walk-up. Brick walls, worn furniture, game-show noise from a small TV. MA, older, brown hair, tired eyes, sits in her chair under an afghan. NARRATOR, late twenties, ordinary build, plain work clothes, stands in the doorway with grocery bags.

(SOUND: TV game-show applause, distant street traffic)

NICK fills the frame as he enters—big, rough-hewn face held together like a puzzle, scars on chin and over left eyebrow, reddish-brown hair uncombed, steel presence. People would move for him in any bar.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

NARRATOR
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That's how old people go downhill.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Real Big Brother. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls.

(SOUND: floorboards creak under Nick's boots)

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Weights clang. A man mid-set on preacher curls sees NICK approach, drops a forty-pound bar, offers the bench. Nick nods, takes a fifty-pound bar like warm-up.

(SOUND: iron plates slam, gym echo)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Milwaukee. Kirk Street. Fifth floor. Vikings in our blood from somewhere over in Viking land. Nick is a modern-day warrior. He runs a line at the metal factory. Drives a burnt-orange 1980 Trans Am with the eagle on the hood.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND KIRK STREET - DAY (FLASH)

Kids' kickball. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Little MARK freezes. Young NICK steps up to furious MRS. ENGEL.

YOUNG NICK
I did it.

(SOUND: glass shatter, then quiet)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never told Ma it was Mark. Gave her the sixty bucks a week later. I was proud.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN - DAY

Pill bottles lined in a careful row on the counter. MA mumbles, glassy-eyed, searching.

(SOUND: bottles rattle, fridge hum)

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
Ma, we don't go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, empty eyes clearing a fraction.

MA
Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don't know what got into me.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I would have to tell Nick. He'll get mad like it was my fault.

EXT. PIZZA PLACE SIDEWALK - DAY

NARRATOR locks his bike. A girl with short brown hair, pale English-story skin, light-blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, blue tennis shoes, backpack—SIONNA—trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: bike chain tick, street noise, soft stumble)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. They look at each other too long.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
Irish goddess. River Shannon.

He is not really listening—only her eyes, sky-before-sunrise blue.

NARRATOR
Want to come in for pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don't eat pizza. But if you want a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She gives a number, walks away, glances back over her shoulder as if memorizing him.

(SOUND: her footsteps fade; distant traffic)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl had ever been interested in me. It was like I knew her. Intense. Like waves at the ocean in New Jersey—the only vacation before Dad left—and I didn't want to move my feet.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers-Bears on TV. NARRATOR at the bar. NICK slaps his back, beer in hand.

(SOUND: crowd roar from TV, pool balls crack, bar murmur)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

NARRATOR
Ma was all confused today. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up. Take her to the store. Do something before she turns into some demented old fart and we pay for a nursing home. I don't have the money for that shit.

Nick's look could punch. Narrator pays, leaves.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hate Nick inside. I'd never tell anybody.

EXT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Cold street. NARRATOR hurries in under old lights on chains, wood floors, classic-literature stacks.

(SOUND: wind, door whoosh, quiet library hush)

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - DESK - NIGHT

LIBRARIAN slides a temporary card. Narrator checks out a worn copy of Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: stamp thump, pages whisper)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when everything was free in here?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S ROOM - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. Narrator reads under a bare bulb—Heathcliff, Catherine. He sets the book down, stares at the ceiling.

(SOUND: pages turn, radiator tick)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I mixed Sionna and Catherine in my dream. Old clothes. Horses. I would end up feeling for her like Heathcliff.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, glasses, notes. SIONNA at a back table—loose green hooded top, washed jeans. Narrator sits. Her eyes again: watery blue crayon.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low indie music, murmur)

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
I know. I read it.

She talks symbols—ghosts, doubles, moors, wild infertile land. Love that never changes destroys.

SIONNA
A love that never changes will destroy you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It reminded me of Nick. Strong. Unwavering. Dumb. Something inside me was coming awake.

They make a dinner date. He walks out lighter.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Narrator toward the bus. City noise.

(SOUND: bus air-brake, footsteps)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left and she never moved on. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him. Nick became my dad when I was eight.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Purple mats. Twenty people. Narrator in T-shirt and shorts, shirt falling over his face until he tucks it. Awkward downward dog, breath hard, dizzy.

(SOUND: instructor's calm voice, collective breath, soft music drone)

SIONNA (after class)
You were really great at that.

NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
You caught on quickly. Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what's going on on your mat.

They smile.

(SOUND: mats rolling, quiet laugh)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
After, I felt calm. Making pizzas didn't bother me. Asshole customers didn't bother me. Best night of work ever.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

NICK stomps, face red, fists clenching. MA sits blank in her chair.

(SOUND: door slam echo, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment, you ass. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That's worth all this. Look at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He is so off the handle. She has herself together.

INT. JOE'S BAR - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

Crowded Saturday. Rock blares. NICK spots ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Nick's eyes go crazy. BOB and SAMMY flank. Narrator tries to step in.

(SOUND: AC/DC from speakers, cue balls, sudden hush)

NICK
What's up, Lindsey? Who's that asshole?

DARK-HAIRED MAN
You got a problem with me? You're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's fists clench. Narrator gets closer than he ever has.

NARRATOR
Nick, leave it. You don't even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're next if you don't get out of my face.

Nick's right fist draws back in slow motion toward Narrator's face. JOE and a BOUNCER rush in. Chaos—head-butt, yells. Narrator runs.

(SOUND: fist whoosh cut short, shout, door crash, heartbeat in ears)

EXT. CITY STREETS - NIGHT

Narrator sprints until his chest burns, cold air knifing lungs—to Sionna's duplex.

(SOUND: pounding feet, ragged breath, distant siren)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Green suede sofa. Narrator collapses; SIONNA holds him.

(SOUND: clock tick, his slowing breath)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I love Nick. He's my brother. He always took care of us.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can't let him stop you from your purpose.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
To find the true self within you, and honor it.

She cups his face. They sleep on the couch. Later a dream-flash: Sionna as angel from lake water; giant Nick with mace shrinks and vanishes.

(SOUND: soft rain on glass; dream wind and water)

INT. FROEDTERT / HOSPITAL CORRIDOR - DAY

Months later compressed to one blow. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces Narrator. Fluorescent glare.

(SOUND: hospital beep distant, shoes on tile)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Serious accident. Werner and Lowe. Your brother Nicholas is in serious condition. Your mother Olivia passed away at the scene. Toxicology pending. He may have been drunk.

Narrator stares through ICU glass at Nick's wild hair, tubes, machines. Turns away.

(SOUND: monitor beep steady; Narrator's breath breaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Empty chair. Afghan folded. Narrator breaks—hands in hair, tears on floor.

(SOUND: raw sobs, apartment silence)

SIONNA enters, holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - LIVING ROOM / HALL - NIGHT

Later. Door knock. NICK—sallow, still damaged, rage lit—pushes in on SIONNA alone. Gray cat slinks. Flower candle and vegetable smell.

(SOUND: hard knock, door chain, cat mew)

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You messed with my brother. He should've been taking care of Ma. You come up with cash and get the fuck away from him.

He backs her to the wall, hand on her throat, switchblade open at collarbone.

NICK
Break it off tonight. One week. Cash. Or this blade. Leak one word—I have friends.

Front door SLAMS open. NARRATOR.

NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick—what the fuck are you doing?

Struggle. Blade bites. Red on her shirt. Nick elbows Narrator, wipes blade, flees.

(SOUND: thunk of door, struggle grunt, wet gasp, footsteps run)

Narrator on the floor with her, hands stacked on the wound near shoulder, blood everywhere, dialing 911 with his chin.

NARRATOR
Stay with me. Stay.

(SOUND: sirens rising, his voice cracking)

INT. HOSPITAL WAITING AREA - NIGHT

Blood on Narrator's clothes. Cops. Later: she lives—punctured lung, shoulder repair. Long recovery. Blank eyes.

(SOUND: distant code tones, HVAC rush)

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months after. SIONNA in the rocking chair, hem of shirt twisting, light gone from the blue eyes.

(SOUND: chair wood creak, quiet room)

SIONNA
I can't feel me anymore. As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe a break. Mom and Dad's. Therapist.

NARRATOR
Stay. We can work it out.

She walks to the bedroom. He leaves for work early—free fall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She blamed me in the end. Not exactly the enlightened being I thought. My fingers slipped off the cliff.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - BELLTOWN - DAY

Years later. Gray blanket sky, mist, hoods not umbrellas. NARRATOR, older, steadier, walks past a keyboard player in ear-flap hat. Tony's Boy's Pizza under a medical building.

(SOUND: light rain hiss, ferry horn distant, keyboard disco-jazz)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years. Chain of pizzerias. Tony's name in honor. I am one of them now—a Seattleite. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. LOWELL'S / PIKE PLACE - DAY

Window seat, ferry on the Sound, cruise ship. Cinnamon roll like a halo. Narrator stares, memory bleeding.

(SOUND: plate set down, market murmur, rain on glass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If I hadn't walked in and held pressure, she would have died in that hallway. Her parents thanked me with eyes that still blamed Nick. He got attempted murder plus the drunk driving that killed Ma. I hope he never gets out. Then she left anyway.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus-lavender. Theme: gratefulness. Narrator on a mat among ten others.

(SOUND: soft chant, breath chorus)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shakespeare—things are neither good nor bad but thinking makes it so. I still felt the void. And sorrow for the bond DNA demands.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Cat BOZO on the couch. Narrator writes a dream in a notebook hard—sage on a green road, dirty pizza box, judgment dropped, joy.

(SOUND: pen scratch, rain, cat purr)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Stop judging. All things illusion. I had to find forgiveness. I booked a flight to Milwaukee.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREETS - DAY

Rental Camry. Old Kirk brick fortress, new dumpsters. Former pizza parlor now Central Cosmetology. Narrator at a deli—reunites with older, thicker BOB.

(SOUND: traffic, deli bell, Bob's loud cackle)

BOB
Holy shit. You came back. You visiting Nick? You got cojones. I couldn't forgive him.

NARRATOR
I need to let the shit go.

BOB
Let it go. I'm proud of you, man. You got out.

Bear hug. Narrator drives past Sionna's old duplex—naked porch, weeds in gravel—then north.

(SOUND: engine, turn signal)

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL - VISITING BOOTH - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. NICK opposite—older, graying, scrawny, bags under sad watery eyes. Fifteen minutes.

(SOUND: door locks boom, muffled voices, phone plastic click)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
I'm here to tell you some things. Just listen. You're an asshole. You killed my mother drunk. You held me down so you could have the big life. You tried to kill the love of my life and drove her away. Now hear the important bit. I forgive you. It sets me free. It releases your control forever.

Nick's tears hit the counter.

NICK
You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You're more than I ever was.

GUARD (O.S.)
Time.

NARRATOR
I want you to get better. I still believe in you. I still love you for being my brother. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I love you.

Nick is led away. Narrator sits with the yellow receiver, light.

(SOUND: chair scrape, locks cycle, his long exhale)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT TERMINAL - DAY

Rolling green hills blur. Then boarding queue. Narrator whispers to himself as the pass scans.

(SOUND: tires on wet road; terminal PA; scanner beep)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somewhere she is happy. I have to believe that. Weight of thirty years—gone.

NARRATOR
(soft, to himself)
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He walks down the jet bridge.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
